      "narrative": "The walk back to the Copper Finch passes under a sky that's begun to spit a thin, half-hearted drizzle. By the time you push through the tavern door, the fine mist has beaded on your cloak and the warmth inside hits you like a wall \u2014 woodsmoke, stale ale, the low hum of voices.\n\nThe Copper Finch hasn't changed in the time you were gone. Copper lamps cast their dim glow over scarred tables. A few patrons nurse morning drinks in the corners, keeping to themselves.\n\nYour old table stands empty. The one near the back wall, where the lamplight doesn't quite reach, where you can watch the room without being watched in return. The wood is worn smooth at the edges from years of elbows.\n\nYou sit.\n\nFor a long moment, nothing happens. The tavern breathes around you \u2014 a cough from the bar, the clink of a glass, the mutter of conversation too low to parse. The fire in the hearth pops once.\n\nThen a figure detaches from the bar. A woman in a charcoal coat, sharp-eyed, moving with the deliberate economy of someone who doesn't waste steps. She crosses to your table and pulls out the chair across from you without asking.\n\nMara Venn settles into the seat, her eyes fixed on yours.\n\n\"You came back,\" she says. Flat. Not a question.\n\n<ref_entity entity_id=\"copper-finch\" alias_used=\"Copper Finch\"></ref_entity>\n<ref_entity entity_id=\"mara-venn\" dialogue_indices=\"[1]\" dialogue_emotions=\"[\"flat\"]\"></ref_entity>..."
